        "evidence": [
          "touch.h declares `bool readTouch(int& sx, int& sy);` on one line, while sibling headers wrap even single-parameter declarations vertically, e.g. display_ui.h's `void uiDrawHome(\\n    const CardInfo& info\\n);` and touch_calibration.h's four-parameter `mapTouchToScreen(...)`.",
          "touch.cpp keeps multi-argument calls on one line throughout (`digitalWrite(TOUCH_MOSI, (out >> bit) & 1);`, `mapTouchToScreen(rawX, rawY, sx, sy);`), whereas every other .cpp in the repo (sd_card.cpp, bench_math.cpp, benchmark.cpp, benchmark_raw.cpp, spi_scale_benchmark.cpp, card_format.cpp, touch_calibration.cpp, ui_geometry.cpp, display_ui.cpp, display_ui_internal.cpp, display_progress.cpp, display_results.cpp, main.cpp) wraps nearly every multi-argument call/declaration to one argument per line."
        ],
        "suggestion": "Reformat touch.h/touch.cpp to the one-argument-per-line style used throughout the rest of src/ (or, if the terser style is actually preferred, apply it project-wide) so this file pair doesn't require a different reading mode than everything else.",
