      "summary": "timedFileWrite/timedFileRead/verifyFile duplicate the same chunked I/O + progress-reporting loop",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "design_coherence",
        "related_files": [
          "src/benchmark/benchmark.cpp"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "timedFileWrite (lines 30-102), timedFileRead (lines 104-176), and verifyFile (lines 178-241) each implement: chunk = min(BENCH_BUFFER_SIZE, BENCH_TEST_SIZE - done); a micros() timing pair; an 'if (result != (int)chunk) return false' guard; done += chunk; and a uiShowProgress() call throttled by nextProgress += BENCH_PROGRESS_STEP.",
          "The only real differences are which SdFat call is made per chunk (file.write vs file.read vs file.read+crc32Update) and the progress title string passed to uiShowProgress()."
        ],
        "suggestion": "Extract one shared chunked-loop helper, e.g. a function that takes the FsFile&, buffer, sizes, a progress title/stage, and a small per-chunk step (function pointer or lambda performing write/read/verify-and-crc), so the chunk-sizing, timing, and progress-throttling logic exists once instead of three times.",
