        ],
        "evidence": [
          "benchmark.cpp line 9: #include \"../ui/display_ui.h\"",
          "runChunkedIo() (benchmark.cpp lines 84-159) calls uiShowProgress() twice, and runQuickBenchmark() calls it two more times (lines 361, 429), making the domain-logic I/O loop directly responsible for driving the TFT progress screen",
          "coupling_matrix shows benchmark->ui: 1 as the only edge that does not point toward core/, and no other benchmark/ file (benchmark_raw.cpp, spi_scale_benchmark.cpp) includes any ui/ header",
          "benchmark_internal.h's own comment states DMA buffer allocation was deliberately kept in hardware/sd_card.h 'since hardware/ can't depend back on benchmark/', showing the team already enforces this direction of layering elsewhere but did not apply the same rule to benchmark->ui"
        ],
        "suggestion": "Have runQuickBenchmark() (and the shared runChunkedIo() helper) accept a progress-callback function pointer instead of calling uiShowProgress() directly -- reuse the exact ChunkIoFn function-pointer idiom already defined in this same file (benchmark.cpp lines 31-36) for a new `using ProgressFn = void(*)(const char*, const char*, size_t, size_t, float);` parameter threaded through runChunkedIo()/timedFileWrite()/timedFileRead()/verifyFile()/runQuickBenchmark(). main.cpp then passes uiShowProgress directly as the callback when it calls runQuickBenchmark(uiShowProgress). This removes benchmark/'s #include of ui/display_ui.h entirely, restoring a strictly one-directional core<-hardware<-{benchmark,ui} dependency graph, without introducing any new abstraction machinery (it mirrors a pattern the file already uses).",
